<ArticleLayout slug="amazon-tmall-jd-one-product-three-listings" ctaLabel="Plan a three-marketplace build" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Amazon publishes its product image requirements where anyone can read them. Tmall and JD do not. Set the three side by side and a finished Amazon build sorts itself into what re-exports, what gets re-cropped, and what has to be built again.</p>

  <h2>Can one Amazon image set serve Tmall and JD?</h2>

  <p>Partly. On the published record, a shadowless Amazon main image re-exports as the Tmall and JD white-background image, and clean additional images re-crop to square for both China carousels. The video and the detail page do not travel: both are rebuilt, once, for both marketplaces. The one-day capture plan hubStudio builds around that split closes this page.</p>

  <h2>How to read these numbers</h2>

  <p>These specs are not read from a seller backend. Both platform rule centers gate their rule text behind a seller login, so no open source reaches the official value.</p>

  <p>What follows is the most commonly published figure for each spec, collected across three separate collections of 18, 15 and 24 independent sources in Chinese and English on 10 September 2026, with the number of sources that agreed shown per row. Counts from different collections are never added together. Rows marked consensus had broad agreement. Rows marked contested did not, and those carry the full spread rather than a single number.</p>

  <p>Treat this as the best available public reading, not as the rule. Platform specs change without announcement and often without an English notice at all. Before you build a batch to any number on this page, open your own seller backend and confirm it there. Reviewed 14 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Amazon is the exception. Its Seller Central help center serves its rules to logged-out readers, and four articles were read on 10 September 2026 and read again on 14 September: the Product image guide, Technical image file requirements, the A+ Content guide and the Shoppable video guide. Each says it applies to selling in the United States, and none prints a date. Amazon rows on this page are primary readings for the US store only. They aren't stretched to any other Amazon store, or to the clothing and multipack detail the guide hands off to separate style guides.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The two platform rule centers a buyer would check are published at public web addresses and serve no rule text without a seller session. On 10 September 2026 the Tmall rule center redirected twice and returned no rule body, and two different JD merchant rule pages returned the rule-center heading and nothing else.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: direct unauthenticated fetches of rule.tmall.com and rule.jd.com, 10 September 2026, four requests across two platforms, redirect chain and response body recorded.</p>

  <h2>Amazon, Tmall and JD image requirements side by side</h2>

  <p>Six rows a production team briefs from. The Amazon column is what the help article says. The Tmall and JD columns give the most commonly published figure and the number of sources that agreed, each count belonging to its own collection.</p>

